=== songha_door
@bg studio_hall t=fade
@show songha neutral at=c
> Songha is standing in front of the door marked 03, reading the glass panel beside it like the small print on a contract.
> OWNER: SONGHA. CONSENT REQUIRED.
@if rooms() >= 1
    songha: You've been through a few of these tonight. What's the success rate?
    you: Nobody's come out worse.
    songha: That's a good metric. I'll take that metric.
songha: Okay. I've thought about it.
songha: First: it's a piece of paper. Worst case, it's a piece of paper with bad handwriting.
songha: Second: seventeen-year-old me had strong opinions. Most of them were about me. I've survived him before.
songha: Third—
> He glances at his watch. You're fairly sure he doesn't actually read it.
songha[thinking]: Third, I don't have a third. I just usually have three.
songha: It's mine, and I'm saying yes. Officially. You can write that down.
songha[smile]: But you're the one standing next to the handle. So "when" is your call.
* [sincere] "Open it."
    @goto songha_room
* [careful] "Not yet."
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: Not yet.
    songha[smile]: That's a real answer. I'll pencil it in.
    > He actually does. *03 — later.* He draws a small box beside the word *later*, so it can be ticked when the time comes.
    songha: Honestly? Slightly relieved. I was about to come up with a fourth point.
    @hub act2

=== songha_room
@bg studio_rooftop t=memory d=1.6
@music songha
@amb wind
@fx stars n=0.6
@cam reveal
> The door doesn't open onto a room. It opens onto the roof.
> This roof. Studio 25's own, only younger. The water tank still has all its paint. The laundromat vent breathes warm air up from four storeys below.
> A plastic chair with one short leg. A crate turned upside down by the tank. The air says spring before anything else does.
@show songha surprised at=c
songha: This is upstairs.
songha: This is two flights from the hall. ECHO built a copy of a place I could have walked to.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 03 — OWNER: SONGHA. TYPE: HANDWRITTEN DOCUMENT, ONE PAGE. DATE: APRIL, FIVE YEARS AGO. 23:12. UNRESOLVED SUBJECTS: 1.
echo: The location is still available. The evening is not. That is the part I provide.
@textbox normal
songha[thinking]: …Okay. Fair.
@if rooms() == 0
    songha: "Unresolved subject." One. Somebody it can't read?
    songha: I was up here alone that night. I'm fairly sure. I'd have written it down if I wasn't.
    > He says it confidently. He says most things confidently. You are starting to learn that it isn't the same as being sure.
@elif rooms() >= 4
    songha: Unresolved subject: one.
    > He turns to you with the calm of a man on the last page of a mystery he solved in chapter two.
    songha: It's you. I have a tally.
    > He does. A tidy row of strokes in the notebook margin, one for every room tonight, with space left over. Of course there's space left over.
    songha[smile]: If the silhouette *isn't* you this time, I'm going to be offended on your behalf.
@else
    songha: One unresolved subject.
    songha[thinking]: Is it you again?
    songha: No, don't answer. Evidence first. I want to do this properly.
songha: Okay. So. First, we—
> He stops. Laughs under his breath, mostly at himself.
songha: I was about to make a plan for walking around a roof.
* [playful] "Go on. What's step one?"
    @rel songha closeness+1
    songha[laugh]: Step one: don't fall off. Step two: see step one. That's the whole plan. It's a good plan.
* [sincere] "We could just look."
    @rel songha trust+1
    songha: …Just look. Right. People do that. I've seen it done.
* [quiet] Walk to the railing and wait for him.
    @rel songha closeness+1
    > After a second, he follows. He stands one careful step further back from the edge than you do, and pretends it's a coincidence.
@hide all
@bg mem_rooftop_stars t=memory d=2
@fx stars n=1
@sfx echo
@textbox memory
> The present thins out. The city below goes softer and older: a few signs that don't exist anymore, a karaoke bar that became a phone shop.
> On the upturned crate sits a seventeen-year-old with too much hair and a notebook balanced on one knee.
> His watch is off, laid flat on the crate beside him. Some habits start early.
> By the railing, a figure made of pale light holds a phone up to the sky.
songha: Is that app any good?
you: It's been loading for ten minutes.
you: I think the sky is buffering.
> The boy snorts. Then looks annoyed that he snorted. He clicks his pen three times, fast.
songha: What are you even looking for?
you: Whether the sky's the same in other cities.
songha: It's the same sky. That's how skies work.
you: The app might disagree. When it loads.
songha: I'm doing a five-year plan. For the Future Letter thing downstairs.
you: Five years? I don't know what I'm doing on Thursday.
songha: That's why you'd need one.
> The light-figure lifts an arm toward a bright star low in the east, faintly orange.
> The reconstruction catches there, like a needle on a scratched record—
@textbox normal
> —and before the figure can speak, the name is already in your mouth.
you: Gerald.
> Beside you, the present-day Songha turns his head. Very slowly.
@textbox memory
you: That one's Gerald. The app's dead, so I'm naming them myself.
songha: That's not its name.
you: Prove it.
> He can't. He looks up at it for a long moment, like a test he didn't revise for.
songha: …Fine. That one's Deadline.
you: Which one?
songha: The one that looks like it's watching you.
> Something small and blinking slides across the dark, left to right, steady as a metronome.
you: And that?
songha: That's a plane.
you: Then it's called Minor Delay.
> The boy laughs, a loud, surprised, badly timed laugh, and claps a hand over his mouth. The building has neighbours.
> Then, quieter, he writes.
songha: Year five. "Learn real star names."
you: Not mine?
songha: Yours are wrong.
you: Mine are better.
songha: They're— {p} yeah, okay. They're better. They're still wrong.
@sfx phone
> The light-figure's phone buzzes. Not a call. An alarm, set in advance.
you: That's me. I have to go and lock up the front desk.
songha: You set an alarm for leaving?
you: I always set one for leaving.
> The boy nods, as if that's the most sensible thing anyone has said to him all week. He's seventeen. It probably is.
songha: The real names. Will you know them by then?
you: Ask me in five years.
> The stairwell door closes on the light. The boy looks at his page for a long time.
> Then, very neatly, he adds something in brackets.
> His watch lies on the crate beside him the whole time. Face up, ticking, completely ignored.
@textbox normal
@show songha surprised at=c
@cam closeup songha
songha: How did you know it was Gerald.
> It isn't quite a question. He has already done the maths. He's waiting for you to show your working.
